<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="ws-four-fours">
  <title>Four Fours</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Group members: <fillin characters="30"/> <fillin characters="30"/></p>
    <p><fillin characters="30"/> <fillin characters="30"/></p>
    <p>Working in your group, write every whole number from 1 to 20 as a calculation that uses exactly four 4's, no more and no less. Record your final answers in the grid at the bottom of the page.</p>
    <p><term>What you may use.</term> The four operations <m>+</m>, <m>-</m>, <m>\times</m>, <m>\div</m>; parentheses; exponents; square roots; decimal points, as in <m>.4</m>; two or more 4's pushed together, as in <m>44</m> or <m>444</m>; and factorials, as in <m>4! = 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1 = 24</m>.</p>
    <p><term>What counts as a 4.</term> Every 4 you write costs one, including the 4 inside <m>\sqrt{4}</m>, the 4 in <m>.4</m>, and each digit of <m>44</m>. The <m>24</m> produced by <m>4!</m> costs only the single 4 you wrote.</p>
    <p><term>More than one answer.</term> Most numbers can be built several different ways. Any numerical expression works as long as it satisfies the conditions.</p>
    <p>Each calculation below uses exactly four 4's. None of them lands in the range 1 to 20, so none of them is an answer you can use, but illustrate what your answers should look like:</p>
    <p>
      <md>
        <mrow>0 \amp = \frac{4-4}{4+4}</mrow>
        <mrow>28 \amp = 4! + 4 + 4 - 4</mrow>
        <mrow>54 \amp = 44 + \frac{4}{.4}</mrow>
        <mrow>64 \amp = \sqrt{4} \cdot \sqrt{4} \cdot 4 \cdot 4</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>Notice the range of tools: the first uses only arithmetic, the second uses a factorial, the third uses both a two-digit 44 and a decimal point, and the fourth uses square roots. My suggestion is to start building calculations and adjusting to see which ones you can get rather than going in order.</p>
  </introduction>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
